# ==========================================================================
#  Q130  Pulling a date apart
# ==========================================================================
#
#  ASKS
#    Break every event date into its parts.
#
#  HOW IT WORKS
#    A xsd:dateTime or xsd:date can be taken apart with YEAR, MONTH, DAY
#    and, where there is a time, HOURS, MINUTES, SECONDS and TIMEZONE. They
#    return plain numbers, which is what makes grouping by month possible
#    at all. SPARQL 1.1 has no date arithmetic beyond this; all three
#    engines here add some, as the diagram shows.
#
#  DIAGRAM
#        "2024-06-15"^^xsd:date
#
#          YEAR   -> 2024        MONTH -> 6        DAY -> 15
#
#        and for a dateTime, three more:
#
#          HOURS  MINUTES  SECONDS      TIMEZONE / TZ
#
#        what you can do with them:
#
#          GROUP BY (MONTH(?d))         events per month
#          FILTER( YEAR(?d) = 2024 )    a year's worth
#          ?d < NOW()                   in the past
#
#        what SPARQL 1.1 does not have, and the engines here do, measured:
#
#          ?d + "P1M"^^xsd:duration     all three   2025-03-08 -> 2025-04-08
#          "P1M"^^xsd:duration + ?d     editor, holos 2025-04-08   fuseki UNBOUND
#          ?a - ?b  (two dates)         all three   a duration, P730D
#          DATEDIFF(?a, ?b)             no such function anywhere
#
#        Date arithmetic is SEP-0002, a proposal for SPARQL 1.2, not 1.1, so
#        an engine without it raises an error and leaves the variable
#        unbound. Put the date first: the other order is not portable even
#        among these three.
#
#        and one thing that was not portable until recently, measured:
#
#          YEAR( "2025-03-08"^^xsd:date )       all three   2025
#                                               (holos UNBOUND up to 0.26.2)
#          YEAR( "...T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ) all three   2025
#
#        SPARQL 1.1 defines the date functions on xsd:dateTime. HOLOS took
#        an xsd:date only from 0.26.3; before that it returned the row with
#        the variable unbound rather than raising -- the failure mode module
#        15 keeps warning about. So this query still promotes the date first:
#
#          STRDT( CONCAT( STR(?date), "T00:00:00" ), xsd:dateTime )
#
#        which works on an engine of any age, and is incidentally the
#        clearest use of STRDT there is.
#
#        q13 computes an age from YEAR() alone rather than subtracting
#        dates. That works on every engine, SEP-0002 or not, and it is off
#        by up to a year -- which the query says.
#
#        +----------------------------------------------------------+
#        |  MONTH returns 6, not "June" and not "06". Formatting a   |
#        |  date for display is not something SPARQL does; do it in  |
#        |  whatever consumes the results.                           |
#        +----------------------------------------------------------+
#
#
#  WHAT TO TAKE AWAY
#    - YEAR, MONTH, DAY, HOURS, MINUTES, SECONDS and TIMEZONE take a date
#      apart into numbers. SPARQL 1.1 promises them only for
#      xsd:dateTime; all three engines here take an xsd:date as well
#      (HOLOS from 0.26.3), and STRDT widens a date for any engine that
#      does not.
#    - Date arithmetic is not SPARQL 1.1: all three engines here add and
#      subtract dates and durations, date first, under SEP-0002. There is
#      no formatting. Grouping and comparison are what the parts are for.
#    - NOW() is fixed for the whole query, so two calls in one query
#      always agree -- which is what makes it usable at all.
#
#  DATA     bookshop-trail-1.1.ttl
#  LOAD IT  https://semantechs.co.uk/turtle-editor-viewer/?dot=https%3A%2F%2Fraw.githubusercontent.com%2Fpwin%2FSPARQL_Course%2Fmain%2Fdata%2Fbookshop-trail-1.1.ttl
#  RUNS ON  editor, holos, fuseki
#  SPEC     SPARQL 1.2 Query 3. RDF Term Constraints
#           https://www.w3.org/TR/sparql12-query/#x3-rdf-term-constraints-informative
#           SPARQL 1.2 Query 10.1 BIND
#           https://www.w3.org/TR/sparql12-query/#x10-1-bind-assigning-to-variables
#  RETURNS  11 rows, on every engine that runs it
# ==========================================================================

PREFIX bs:  <https://example.org/bookshop-trail/schema#>
PREFIX xsd: <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#>

SELECT ?year ?month (COUNT(*) AS ?events)
WHERE {
  ?event a           bs:Event ;
         bs:eventDate ?date .
  # xsd:date, promoted to xsd:dateTime so that every engine can read
  # it -- see the diagram. STRDT builds a typed literal from a string.
  BIND( STRDT( CONCAT( STR(?date), "T00:00:00" ), xsd:dateTime ) AS ?at )
  BIND( YEAR(?at)  AS ?year )
  BIND( MONTH(?at) AS ?month )
  BIND( DAY(?at)   AS ?day )
}
GROUP BY ?year ?month
ORDER BY ?year ?month
